// Generated by scripts/generate-skill.mjs.
export const SKILL_PROMPT = {"name":"lightroom-agent","title":"Lightroom agent","description":"Operate Lightroom Classic through the lightroom-agent tools and make photographic decisions with them - find and cull photos, judge sharpness, develop RAWs, mask, crop, build looks and presets, export, and verify every change on Lightroom's own render. Use whenever you are about to read or change a Lightroom catalog through these tools, or when someone asks to select, critique, edit, compare, prepare or export their photographs in Lightroom, even if they do not name the tools."};
export const SKILL_TEXT = "# SKILL.md\n\n---\nname: lightroom-agent\ndescription: Operate Lightroom Classic through the lightroom-agent tools and make photographic decisions with them - find and cull photos, judge sharpness, develop RAWs, mask, crop, build looks and presets, export, and verify every change on Lightroom's own render. Use whenever you are about to read or change a Lightroom catalog through these tools, or when someone asks to select, critique, edit, compare, prepare or export their photographs in Lightroom, even if they do not name the tools.\n---\n\n# Lightroom agent\n\nThe tools can change everything in someone's catalog, and Lightroom's history is shared with them.\n\nThe tool descriptions and the server instructions are the authority on what each tool does. This skill tells you how to combine them well and how to judge photographs; when the two seem to disagree, follow the tool description and say so.\n\n## Ground rules\n\n- **Keep a way back before any Develop change.** Make a snapshot (`create_develop_snapshot`) or a named virtual copy (`create_virtual_copies` with `copy_name`), and check that it exists. There is no batch snapshot: for many photos, snapshot the representative, run the transfer with `dry_run` first, and keep each target's previous values from `get_develop_settings` (one call for the set), or edit named virtual copies (up to 25 per call) and leave the masters untouched. Snapshots do not store ratings, flags, labels or metadata: note those separately if you will change them.\n- **Anything that deletes, removes, overwrites or replaces the person's work needs their word for that action first.** Examples: `remove_photos_from_catalog`, `reset_develop_adjustments`, `delete_collection`, `remove_from_collection`, `delete_develop_snapshot`, `apply_develop_snapshot` (it overwrites the current edit), `delete_masks`, `delete_empty_masks`, `delete_spot`, `undo_last_step`, `set_process_version`, keyword subtree removal, a full `copy_develop_settings` (it replaces or removes the target's masks) and exports over existing files. Files on disk are never yours to delete. A request to \"prepare\" or \"improve\" photos is not permission for any of these.\n- **Write within the request.** Selection is not development, development is not publication. Ratings, flags and labels carry the person's meaning: read the existing ones first and change them only as asked.\n- **One writer.** Develop calls run one at a time across every client of the server; do not fire parallel writes, and do not work on a photo the person is editing at that moment.\n- **Verify by reading back and looking.** A `done` reply is necessary, not sufficient: read the changed values back and look at a fresh render before you report a change.\n- **Say what you did not check.** Preview size, unverified full-resolution detail, a mask edge you did not inspect: name them.\n\n## Before the first call\n\n1. Check which tools the session exposes. Toolsets are fixed when the server starts (`LIGHTROOM_MCP_TOOLSETS`: core, develop, masks or all); read-only mode (`LIGHTROOM_MCP_READ_ONLY`) hides every write, exports and `get_photo_detail` included. If a tool is missing, check the server's toolsets, read-only mode and version before deciding it is unsupported, and tell the person.\n2. Identify the actual source: a folder (`list_folders`, `get_folder_photos`), a collection (`list_collections`, `get_collection_photos`), the selection (`get_selected_photos`, which falls back to the filmstrip) or a search. Address photos by their catalog ids; paths are ambiguous with virtual copies.\n3. Read what is already there: `get_photo_metadata` (ratings, flags, labels, virtual-copy and stack identity), `get_develop_settings`, `get_ai_metadata` for earlier private notes.\n\n## Read every reply's outcome\n\nEvery tool answers `{outcome, data, error, failed, unknown, not_found, remaining, warnings}`. The class comes from what the call wrote, not from the kind of error.\n\n| outcome | meaning | your next step |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `done` | everything asked happened and was confirmed | verify the effect as usual |\n| `partial` | `data` holds what happened, the lists what did not | resend exactly `remaining`; fix and retry `failed` items; read `unknown` items first; never repeat what succeeded |\n| `failed` | nothing changed (Lightroom's history may still hold the write and its undo as two steps) | read the reason, fix the cause, retry is safe |\n| `unknown` | something may have been written; the error names what | read the photo's current state before anything else; a blind retry duplicates |\n\n`warnings` never change the outcome but often matter (a mask placed through Upright, a mask shown again, a key a JPEG cannot store). Details, budgets and timeouts: [tools](references/tools.md#outcomes-ordering-and-time).\n\n## Judge pictures on the right evidence\n\n- **A preview's size is not the detail it holds.** Previews come from Lightroom's preview cache, which can answer with an enlargement of a smaller cached preview, a cropped photo most of all, without saying so, and every whole-frame preview is a downscale. A preview can hide a slight smear or defocus and can make a sharp frame look soft; streaks or doubled contours across the subject are strong evidence of motion, but check a neighbouring frame or a detail crop when unsure.\n- **Reject from a preview only a gross smear:** motion streaks, doubled edges, a subject blurred where the same preview holds sharper areas. A frame that only looks uniformly soft is uncertain, not rejected.\n- **Everything finer comes from `get_photo_detail`:** whether a frame is critically sharp, which of two near-identical frames is sharper, and any keep that rests on sharpness. Each detail call renders the full photo and leaves one Export history step and the exported mark on it, so ask for all regions of a photo in one call.\n- **Look again after every change** on a fresh preview.\n\n## The workflow\n\n1. **Name the job and how it will be checked:** scope, selection or development, intended output and its size, what must stay true (skin, product colour, a building's proportions), any stop after a pass.\n2. **Cull** whole frames first, then detail crops for the candidates whose verdict depends on sharpness; two separate gates, technical and editorial: [cull](references/cull.md).\n3. **Develop one representative per lighting situation** in small, rendered passes before anything is copied across a set: [development](references/development.md).\n4. **Mask** only where a global change cannot do the job, and check each mask on the render: [masks](references/masks.md).\n5. **Transfer** only what is truly shared (a look, not one photo's white balance, crop or masks), then read back and render every target.\n6. **Report** per photo by filename and catalog id: decision or treatment, what changed, the trade-off, preview resolution, what was not verified, and whether you stopped at a requested pause.\n\n## Load the reference for the branch you are on\n\n- Any tool call beyond a simple read, an outcome you do not understand, crop, black and white, snapshots, presets, exports: [tools](references/tools.md).\n- Any mask: creation, AI masks, values, positions, admission: [masks](references/masks.md).\n- Selecting frames, judging sharpness, sequences, readiness for publication: [cull](references/cull.md).\n- Developing a RAW, the closed loop of passes, copying settings across a set: [development](references/development.md).\n- Composition, crop, colour and light decisions on a new set: [foundations](references/foundations.md).\n- Birds and animals: [wildlife](references/wildlife.md). Buildings, cities, landscapes, Upright: [architecture and landscape](references/architecture-landscape.md). People, products, food: [portrait and product](references/portrait-product.md).\n- Looks, Color Grading, Glow, black and white, building and saving a preset: [looks and presets](references/looks.md).\n- Preparing photos for a named destination, formats, export settings: [publishing](references/publishing.md).\n\nThe references carry their findings inline; their sources are provenance, not required reading. Observations marked \"observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0\" were made on that version and can change with Lightroom updates; recheck with `describe_develop_keys` and a render when a result surprises you.\n\n\n# references/architecture-landscape.md\n\n# Architecture, city and landscape\n\nTell a descriptive architectural image from an expressive one, and an atmospheric landscape from a demand for maximum detail.\n\n## Geometry has different causes\n\nCamera position sets the relative size of near and far things; a lens profile cannot restore a distant mountain's size. Camera tilt makes verticals converge. Barrel or pincushion distortion bends straight lines. Rectilinear projection stretches shapes near the edges of a wide frame even when lines are straight. Diagnose which one you face before correcting. Establish true lines first: a sloping street is not a horizontal, trees on a hillside need not be vertical.\n\nFor a descriptive facade, compare parallel verticals and faithful proportions; for a view up a tower, convergence can carry the height. After a correction check window shapes, width, people and circles at the edges, the roof and base, and the context. Correcting perspective costs image area and resolution at the stretched corners, and it does not move the camera: leave a margin around the subject when shooting.\n\n## Upright and Projection\n\nUpright modes, written as `PerspectiveUpright`: 1 auto (verticals and horizontals while keeping as much of the image as possible), 2 full (both, the strongest correction and crop), 3 level (horizon only), 4 vertical (verticals only), 0 off. Guided is not available through the tools. Lightroom applies the lens profile first, so turn it on before Upright. On test images (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0) auto left part of a convergence, full and vertical made edges vertical, level levelled a horizon, and on a grid without real structure every mode rotated the frame wildly: weak or misleading lines produce a wrong correction.\n\nProcedure (verify each step on the render):\n1. **Decide whether to correct.** A frame without clear straight lines (trees, mountains, a curved interior) gets no Upright.\n2. **Base first:** snapshot, lens profile and Remove Chromatic Aberration on, and keep the crop from `get_crop`.\n3. **Mode:** vertical for a building shot tilted up with a roughly level horizon; auto if vertical crops too much or bends the horizontals; full when verticals and horizontals both converge, expecting the largest crop; level when only the horizon is tilted and the verticals should keep their convergence.\n4. **After every Upright write** read back the setting and the crop, and look at a fresh `review` preview: verticals parallel or with the intended residual, no rotation or warp, blank wedges at the edges, roof and base kept, edges not stretched. Take a detail crop of a top corner: the stretch also softens it.\n5. **Crop away the blank edges** with `set_crop` and `relative_to: \"crop\"` placed on the preview. The tool does not undo Upright, so the frame can sit up to about 0.05 away near the edges: leave a margin and adjust once.\n6. **Manual fallback** when no mode looks right or a partial correction looks more natural: Upright off, then `PerspectiveVertical`, set from the image rather than a fixed value, `PerspectiveAspect` against a stretched look, `PerspectiveScale` to pull blank edges inside, `PerspectiveRotate` for a small tilt. A slight residual convergence often looks more natural than geometric parallels. Read the render after each.\n\n**Projection** (`ReshapeAmount`, new in Lightroom Classic 16.0) reshapes the edges and corners of a wide frame to reduce the stretch of faces and round objects near the corners; on a facade it did not straighten verticals. Use it for a person, animal or round object stretched near the edge; do not use it as perspective correction or on descriptive architecture or a horizon near the edge, where it bends lines that must stay straight.\n\n## Depth and atmosphere\n\nName the foreground, middle and far planes and their light. Support existing diagonals, light corridors, silhouettes, lit slopes and mist layers. Equal Clarity or Dehaze on every plane erases depth; a dark defined foreground against a soft distance may be the point of a fog picture.\n\nBuild the broad brightness hierarchy before local refinement. The sky is not simply the top half: inspect branches, antennas, bright buildings and ridge lines. Opening the land can leave a bright rim; darkening the sky can leave dark halos, grey twigs or an unnatural horizon. Toggle the effect at whole-frame and edge scale; reduce a correction whose edge dominates before refining it. Recheck colour and brightness after Dehaze. A daylight sky normally stays brighter than the land.\n\nKeep sharpening off sky and smooth walls with Masking; use Texture rather than Clarity for foliage, and avoid strong Clarity on smooth skies unless the effect is intended. Water reflects: glints and reflected colour may dominate locally, and a specular highlight need not contain texture. Watch foliage for crunchy detail, uniform greens and oversaturated planes.\n\nFor blue hour protect the difference between the cool surroundings and warm windows; auto white balance can neutralise the very blue that makes the scene. A coloured source need not become white.\n\n## Directions to test\n\n| Direction | Preserve or strengthen | Stop or revise when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Descriptive architecture | Proportions, material, controlled colour | Crop or stretch misrepresents the building |\n| Dramatic geometry | Intentional convergence, diagonals, tonal hierarchy | The tilt looks accidental or shapes collapse |\n| Airy landscape | Soft distance, light air, a readable anchor | The scene becomes shapeless grey |\n| Light relief | Existing sun and shadow, separated planes | Masks invent incompatible light or show |\n| Blue hour | Cool ambient with warm inhabited light | Everything turns blue, or windows become orange blobs |\n| Graphic monochrome | Form, rhythm, tonal plane separation | The colour story is lost or forms merge |\n\nExclude photo-specific geometry from any colour look you intend to reuse.\n\n## Several frames\n\nPanorama and HDR need several suitable originals; virtual copies of one RAW add neither coverage nor dynamic range. The tools have no merge route: use Lightroom's Photo Merge in the UI when the person asks for one.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Cambridge in Colour: wide-angle lenses](https://cdn.cambridgeincolour.com/tutorials/wide-angle-lenses.htm): viewpoint, tilt, distortion and edge stretch.\n- [Adobe: Upright](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/guided-upright-perspective-correction.html); [Adobe: masking](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/masking.html); [Adobe: panoramas](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/panorama.html).\n- [David Chapman: landscape perspective](https://www.naturettl.com/perspective-in-landscape-photography/); [Mikko Lagerstedt: fog and mist](https://www.mikkolagerstedt.com/blog/how-to-photograph-fog-and-mist/); [Anton Gorlin: Dehaze](https://antongorlin.com/blog/how-to-use-photoshop-and-lightroom-dehaze/); [Tim Grey: blue-hour white balance](https://asktimgrey.com/2021/10/19/white-balance-for-blue-hour/).\n\n\n# references/cull.md\n\n# Cull: selecting frames on the right evidence\n\nThe target is a set of frames that hold up for their intended presentation, not merely recognisable subjects. Do not invent a numeric quality score; give each frame a reason.\n\n## Name the viewing contract\n\nBefore judging, state the output (a web post, a print, a client delivery), its **delivery size** (the pixel size the destination shows), the intended set and the critical content of each picture: an eye, a gesture, an architectural line, a material, an atmosphere.\n\nThe whole frame and the critical detail answer different questions. A whole-frame view (`review` preview or an export) shows composition, tone, colour, mask seams and halos. It never establishes fine sharpness, and a larger whole frame tempts the judge to skip detail crops without showing more. Two more limits: preview regions are cut from a capped render, and the client may downscale images before the model sees them, so account for the size you actually receive rather than the size you asked for.\n\nFor any crop you judge, record the render size and the crop's pixel box, and the scale at which you actually viewed it.\n\n## The procedure\n\n1. **Whole-frame pass at `cull` (864 px).** Judge subject, moment and composition. Reject only failures visible at that size: a gross smear as [SKILL.md](SKILL.md#judge-pictures-on-the-right-evidence) defines it, a cluttered frame, a closed eye where the moment needs an open one. Done when every frame is rejected with a reason visible at 864 px, marked for a detail crop, or marked uncertain.\n2. **Detail crop** with `get_photo_detail`, regions placed on the cull preview in the same normalised coordinates. Which region:\n   - eye-led subjects (animals, people): the nearest eye with a ring of feathers, fur or skin about three to five eye diameters wide, so the eye-ring edge, the catchlight and the nearest hair or feather structure are in one box;\n   - a picture led by something else (flight shape, behaviour, a back view, a building edge): the detail that carries it;\n   - a second or third region only when the verdict depends on it: the other subject in an interaction, a bright edge where fringing is suspected, a smooth area to judge noise.\n\n   Typical eye boxes are 400 to 1024 px. Done when every marked frame has its crops, with render size and pixel boxes recorded.\n3. **Decide the technical gate at delivery scale.** The crop shows the render at 1:1; the destination shows it at s = delivery long edge ÷ render long edge. Crisp at 1:1: passes, and stays usable for a tighter crop. Soft at 1:1: reduce the saved crop by s (any image tool that resamples) and judge again; it passes only if the criterion holds there, recorded as \"passes at delivery scale only\", and any tighter crop or format variant needs a new check. Soft at delivery scale: fails. (Untested as a rule.)\n4. **Sequences.** Compare the frames' detail crops side by side; they share the 1:1 scale, so magnification matches when subject distance is similar. For a short selection, the default in a **weak sequence** (most frames questionable) is the best one or two that pass the technical gate, and none when none passes; when most frames are good, keep every variation that differs in gaze, behaviour, shape, interaction or setting. An archive or documentary brief may keep more: agree the rule with the person. Similarity alone does not make a good frame expendable.\n\nThe detail render has no output sharpening, so a sharpened export looks slightly crisper. When `get_photo_detail` is unavailable, leave fine sharpness unverified and say so: in a read-only session exports are blocked too, and a refusal for another reason needs its cause checked first. In a writable session where the person allows exports, one full-resolution export cut without resampling gives the same evidence.\n\n## Diagnose what the frame contains\n\nCompare subject detail, a smooth area, the focus planes and the sharpest nearby frame before accepting heavy noise reduction or sharpening: noise reduction can make missing detail look clean, and sharpening can put a crisp edge around a soft form.\n\n| Observation | Candidate explanation and the decisive comparison |\n| --- | --- |\n| Random fine luminance or colour variation over real structure | Noise: compare a smooth area and the structure before and after NR |\n| Smooth transitions, missing fine structure, no direction | Defocus: compare focus planes and a sharper neighbour |\n| Directional traces, doubled edges | Camera or subject motion: does the surrounding share the direction? |\n| Sharp eye, progressively soft near and far anatomy | Depth of field: does the required content still read? |\n| Low contrast behind foreground mesh, leaves or flare | Obstruction or scatter: compare positions and the baseline rather than assume a focus error |\n\nThese are clues, not certain classifications. **For selection the cause does not matter:** critical detail that is soft at delivery scale fails whatever the cause. Diagnose the cause for capture lessons and for the one correctable case, a contrast veil from obstruction or flare that Dehaze or local contrast may reduce. Do not blame a lens from one photograph. Shutter speed in EXIF is a cheap prior for which frames to check first (slower than about 1/500–1/1000 s for a perched bird, 1/2000 s for action), never proof either way.\n\n## Two gates\n\n**Technical gate:** at delivery size the subject reads, critical detail is sufficient for the intended picture, and the treatment has no damaging halos, plastic texture, clipping, mask seams or geometry losses. For an eye-led subject the nearest eye at delivery scale shows a crisp eye-ring edge, a compact catchlight and resolved hair or feathers around it; a stretched or doubled catchlight signals motion. A catchlight is a strong positive, not a hard rule. A clear silhouette or intentional motion can pass without a sharp visible eye when that is the picture's purpose. Recognisability alone does not pass a detail-led portrait.\n\n**Editorial gate:** the frame offers a strong moment, form, light, relationship or place and earns its position in the set. A technically fine but weaker duplicate can fail it; a rare moment can justify a documented technical compromise. Being the best of its sequence, or time already spent developing it, does not guarantee a place.\n\nVerdicts: **ready** after both gates pass; **needs work** when a specific feasible correction or a missing check could settle it; **exclude for this use** when the limitation defeats the intended use. Give technical and editorial reasons separately. An exclusion for one use is not a catalog decision: apply Reject, flags and labels by the person's own scheme and the request, and keep your reasons in `set_ai_metadata` when the person wants them recorded.\n\nKeep three things apart in your notes, because they change for different reasons: the selection verdict, the editing need (none, minor global, local work), and how sure you are.\n\n## Bounded verification\n\nFor each candidate: one current whole-frame view and the necessary detail crops; a sharper neighbour or baseline when that decides it. Re-inspect after a material correction or an unresolved discrepancy, not indefinitely. Stop when every requested frame has bounded evidence and the uncertain ones are named.\n\n\n# references/development.md\n\n# Development: a closed loop on Lightroom's own render\n\nJudge colour and tone only on Lightroom's render of the RAW (a preview or an export with current settings). A camera JPEG beside the RAW can help with composition, focus and expression, not with colour.\n\n## Terms\n\n- **Base development:** the per-image correction that makes a RAW look right before any look: profile, white balance, tone, detail, lens and geometry. It belongs to one photo or one consistent scene.\n- **Look** and **preset:** see [looks](references/looks.md); a preset can carry a look or a base, and its name does not say which.\n- **Lab copy:** a virtual copy made only to test a treatment, kept apart from the master's edit.\n\n## Cluster before editing\n\nGroup the set by lighting and intent, not by folder or event: open shade, direct sun, backlight, warm or mixed interior light, stage or night light, high ISO. Pick one representative per cluster with ordinary light for that cluster, and keep difficult outliers separate. Do not build one universal treatment for visibly different light.\n\n## Passes on the representative\n\nPreserve the starting edit first (snapshot or named virtual copy). Then change one concern per pass and look at a fresh `review` preview after each:\n\n1. **Baseline.** Read the settings, take a preview, write down the intent in one sentence and what must stay true.\n2. **Technical correction.** White balance, Exposure, Highlights, Shadows, Whites, Blacks: usable exposure, protected highlights that hold information, plausible shadows. No creative colour yet. Lens profile and Remove Chromatic Aberration belong here, as part of the camera and lens base.\n3. **Geometry.** Upright or manual transform, then the final crop ([architecture and landscape](references/architecture-landscape.md#upright-and-projection), [foundations](references/foundations.md#intent-attention-and-frame)). Solve geometry before the final crop.\n4. **Tonal shape.** Contrast, curve, local relationships. Describe the tonal goal (soft highlight roll-off, deep but open blacks) before setting numbers.\n5. **Colour.** White balance refinements, then bounded HSL changes on visible subjects (skin, foliage, sky, neutrals). Do not map a scene label to arbitrary HSL values.\n6. **Local light.** Masks where a global change cannot do it ([masks](references/masks.md)).\n7. **Look,** if one is wanted ([looks](references/looks.md)).\n8. **Detail and noise.** Judged on detail crops of the final state: sharpening, Texture, noise reduction. Low light is a modifier on any look, not a look.\n\nMake one or two render-and-inspect cycles per pass. Stop when changes oscillate, the visible gain becomes negligible, or what remains is local or a matter of the person's taste; for the latter, show alternatives instead of deciding. Keep a short log per pass: values before and after, what improved, what got worse, accepted or reverted.\n\n## Order of AI and Enhance work\n\nRun Denoise early, before AI masks and Content-Aware Remove, then revisit sharpening and Texture (Adobe's guidance since Lightroom Classic 12.3). Denoise computes in the background after the tool reports applied. Do not run heavy processing across an unculled shoot by habit: cull first, then spend compute on the frames that stay. More pixels from Super Resolution are not more optical detail.\n\n## Detail without damage\n\nInspect the eye or key detail, fine structure and a smooth area before accepting noise reduction. The amount depends on exposure, shadow lift, subject size and file, not only ISO; preserve structure rather than maximise cleanness. Texture works on mid-scale detail and barely raises noise; Clarity acts on broader structure and shifts tone and saturation and can look gritty. Sharpening's Masking slider keeps sharpening off smooth areas (sky, walls, skin) but a high value also withholds it from fine hair and feathers; lower Masking plus negative Texture in a background mask is the usual alternative. No slider restores focus. Judge sharpening on the final state, because mask writes on a RAW re-render it at texture level ([masks](references/masks.md#mask-writes-re-render-a-raw)).\n\n## Highlights, shadows and mixed light\n\nA Develop histogram shows the current rendering, not sensor clipping. A reversible Exposure or Highlights test shows whether bright detail and plausible colour remain; blank white turning blank grey is not recovered detail. When information is absent, consider allowed clipping, a silhouette, another crop or another frame. Lifting every shadow flattens the scene and shows noise; shadow visibility is not the objective. Extreme highlight recovery with strong shadow lifting can flatten tonal separation into the grey \"HDR\" look: inspect the result.\n\nChoose white balance by the scene's intent: a fixed Kelvin number is not a transferable mood. Mixed sources can keep their character when the main subject is convincing; one white balance cannot neutralise every source, so correct an interfering cast locally when it matters.\n\n## Copy across the cluster\n\nShow the representative's before and after, and get agreement when the direction is the person's choice, before copying. Then copy only the keys meant for the cluster: `copy_develop_settings` with a `settings` list, or a preset made from the look ([looks](references/looks.md#make-a-preset-from-a-tuned-look)). Exposure, white balance, crop, transform, lens, masks and detail are per photo unless the light is truly the same; evaluate them on each target. Read back every target and look at its render; if several targets need major rescue, reconsider the cluster or the look; handle an isolated outlier separately.\n\n## Final check\n\nRead back changed values and snapshot or copy identity, then look at a fresh whole-frame render plus enough subject detail: mask edges, clipping, colour contamination, geometry and crop losses, noise and halos, at the delivery size. Do not repeat a successful check without a new change or concern. Report as [SKILL.md](SKILL.md#the-workflow) says, separating executed changes from proposals.\n\n\n# references/foundations.md\n\n# Foundations: intent, frame, colour and light\n\nKeep the truthfulness the picture's use requires (documentary, product, likeness); everything else is a creative choice made deliberately.\n\n## Intent, attention and frame\n\nDescribe the particular picture in one sentence: what draws attention first, what follows, and the mood. Ground it in visible light, gesture, form, rhythm, relationship or place. Name what must stay recognisable or accurate: expression, markings, product colour, proportions, weather. Fix the largest obstacle to that intent first.\n\nThree quick checks make it concrete: view the frame in greyscale to find subject and background of equal brightness that merge; squint or view it small to see the three to five main tonal masses; rank what pulls attention (brightest area, strongest local contrast, sharpest detail, most saturated colour, faces and eyes). A bright corner, a sign, a red garment or a high-contrast branch can outcompete the subject: quiet the competitor before pushing every subject slider. Background can carry scale, habitat and story; a cleaner crop costs that context.\n\nCheck the edges for tangencies, severed limbs, cut handles, stray reflections and distracting fragments. Space toward a gaze or motion, centred symmetry, negative space and deliberate tightness are choices with different effects; a thirds grid is one possibility. Choose the aspect ratio for the picture's structure; a shared series ratio helps presentation but can cost individual compositions, so see that cost before imposing it. Establish a reliable horizontal or vertical before straightening: a sloping street, a curved bank or a leaning body is not one.\n\n**Comparing crops.** Keep the current frame as the baseline and test a purposeful alternative: reduce a competing edge, give the eye or gesture useful space, strengthen a diagonal, symmetry or reflection. Thirds and golden-ratio lines suggest positions; keep a guide only when the visible relationships improve. Check the complete subject, wings and tail, gaze room and reflection. Compare at the intended display size and check critical detail again: fewer pixels and a larger displayed subject can expose softness even when the composition improves. An unchanged crop is a valid result.\n\n## Colour and tone\n\nJudge hue, saturation and lightness together with a colour's area and position. Choose the strongest useful separation: light/dark, warm/cool, vivid/quiet or hue. Analogous colours unify; opposing colours separate. Colour symbolism depends on the scene and the viewer.\n\nName the control colours and how far each may move: skin, white fur or feathers, food, fabric, neutral walls, packaging. White balance sets the global warm/cool and green/magenta balance; a neutral eyedropper needs a truly neutral, unclipped area.\n\nThe profile interprets the RAW's tone and colour: compare foundations before complex grading, and revisit the treatment if you change profile later. HSL moves colour families and reaches subject and background alike; Color Grading targets tonal ranges; RGB curves change tone and colour by another mechanism. None of them is the same as raising white balance. Keep a tonal hierarchy: recover highlights that hold information, keep depth in blacks where it serves, and do not make every shadow equally visible. Matte blacks reduce dark separation; strong Dehaze can erase atmospheric depth and shift colour.\n\n## Light, material and alternatives\n\nWhen the intent is natural, support the existing light locally: lighten a face, restrain a competing stone, shape a material's reflection. Strong relighting is a creative choice: name it honestly. Material priorities differ: skin, rock, feather, ceramic and a smooth background each want different texture, sharpening and noise treatment.\n\nWhen the direction is open, compare two distinct ideas, and a third only for an independent purpose. Show them at similar size and broadly similar brightness, and name the benefit and cost of each (clearer face but weaker place; calmer greens but less colour variety). For a series, match relationships in contrast, highlights, saturation and texture while letting real light and white balance differ. Stop when the intent reads and the required qualities survive.\n\n## Output\n\nCheck the whole frame, the important detail and the actual delivery size: reductions alter fine lines, text, noise and sharpening. Use the destination's size and colour requirements; sRGB is a common web choice, not a substitute for a requested specification. For print use the lab's paper profile and soft-proof where available; a screen simulation cannot guarantee another display or a print.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Mareike Keicher: applying colour harmonies](https://www.captureone.com/blog/applying-color-harmonies-in-capture-one): purpose, protected colours and colour weight (Capture One controls are not Lightroom's).\n- [Kevin Morgans: wildlife composition](https://www.naturettl.com/composition-wildlife-photography/): subject versus environment.\n- [Adobe: tone and colour](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/image-tone-color.html); [Julieanne Kost: profiles](https://jkost.com/blog/2024/07/the-power-of-profiles-in-lightroom-classic.html); [Max Wendt: Texture](https://blog.adobe.com/en/publish/2019/05/14/from-the-acr-team-introducing-the-texture-control); [Adobe: colour management](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/workspace/color-management.html).\n\n\n# references/looks.md\n\n# Looks and presets\n\nA **look** is a visual goal, a **profile** is how the RAW is interpreted, and a **preset** is a stored group of settings.\n\n## Define a look by its goals\n\nWrite the look as goals before numbers: how skin, greens, sky and neutrals should read, highlight roll-off, how open the shadows are, contrast, saturation, grain. When the person has reference images they approve, compare target and reference on exactly those points. Keep technical conditions apart from the look: low light, high ISO or mixed light are modifiers that change noise and white-balance handling under any look, not looks of their own. Do not promote one frame's slider values into a look's definition without seeing them work on other frames.\n\n## Build and compare\n\nChoose a representative frame with ordinary light for the set, then test the look on it and on at least one meaningfully different frame before calling it transferable. Use a light-faithful baseline to show what the look changes. An alternative earns its place by a distinct visual purpose, not a different name. Compare at the same framing and similar apparent brightness; name each one's benefit and cost. Values without a rendered comparison are proposals.\n\nDirections to audition, not recipes; invent another when it serves the picture better:\n\n| Direction | Useful relationship | Main risk |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Light-faithful colour | Existing light, believable material colour | Stays flat if the hierarchy is unresolved; neutral is not automatically good |\n| Warm faded film | Soft highlight transition, lifted blacks, restrained greens, warm light | Orange cast, weak dark separation |\n| Deep calm greens | Quiet foliage, strong subject separation | Uniform dead greens, shifted green subjects, cut-out edges |\n| Cool shadows, amber light | Warm light against a cooler environment | Cyan whites or skin, muddy shadows, teal-orange everywhere |\n| Cool stone | Moderate contrast, quiet saturation for concrete and overcast city | Dead brick, wood or evening light |\n| Open colour | Gently opened midtones, restrained vibrance for travel and landscape | Lost distance, noisy shadows, loud sky |\n| Soft portrait | Gentle highlight curve, restrained saturation, distinct skin and materials | Grey lips, flat face, washed clothing |\n| Soft pastel, airy | Low visual density, gentle transitions | Weak subject, chalky highlights |\n| Graphic high contrast | Shape, silhouette, decisive light and dark masses | Blocked detail, excessive edge contrast |\n| Atmospheric low key | Depth, selective light, night or subdued habitat | The gesture disappears |\n| Sculptural monochrome | Gesture, light, form, texture | Colours merge into one grey |\n\nFor film-inspired work, name the visible qualities you are after; curves and grain are not a faithful emulation of a film stock.\n\n## Color Grading\n\nWheels for shadows, midtones, highlights and global, each with hue, saturation and luminance. Balance moves the boundary between the shadow and highlight tints; Blending (default 50) sets how much they overlap without making them stronger; neither affects the global wheel. Grading only adds colour and cannot desaturate; small saturations already show. Adobe's order: white balance and base tone first, then only the wheels you need, then Balance and Blending, global last if at all. Key names are in [tools](references/tools.md#write-develop-settings). Starting points to verify on the render: saturations of about 5–15 per wheel; for a warm/cool split, shadow hue around 200–230 and highlight hue around 40–55.\n\nHow a look divides between controls: the composite curve carries tone (fade, contrast, roll-off); the wheels carry the colour of each tonal band; HSL carries desaturation and hue shifts of colour families; per-channel curves stay for a tint pinned to a precise tonal point, such as a cross-processed crossover. Do not correct a colour cast with the wheels: that is white balance or a local correction. Color Grading transfers in a preset and through `match_photo_style`; white balance stays per photo.\n\n## Glow (Lightroom Classic 16.0)\n\nGlow diffuses highlights and makes light sources bloom; Range sets which tones it reaches, Spread how far it radiates, Warmth its colour. Styles: diffusion (an overall soft, misty rendering that lowers highlight contrast), bloom (light sources and speculars that radiate), halation (a warm film-like halo around bright edges against dark), motion and streak (directional streaks, set with `GlowDirection`). Inside masks only the amount is per mask; style, range, spread and warmth are shared with the global Glow.\n\nWhere it serves: sunrise and sunset, backlit or rim-lit scenes, glints on water, a window or street light at blue hour, airy or low-key looks. Do not use it on a detail-led portrait or wildlife frame as a global effect (it lowers micro-contrast on exactly the bright edges the technical gate reads), on descriptive architecture, to hide softness or noise, or on clipped highlights, which it spreads. Start low (amount about 10–25, Range low, Spread as the main control) and compare with and without on the whole frame and on a detail crop of the brightest important detail.\n\n## Black and white\n\nIt serves when gesture, form, texture and light carry the picture and colour adds nothing or distracts: clashing colours in the surroundings, a grey day, mixed light no white balance settles, rim light and silhouettes, fur and feather pattern, architectural rhythm. It does not serve when colour is the subject, or when subject and background differ only in hue at the same lightness.\n\n1. Convert a copy with `set_treatment` `black_and_white` ([tools](references/tools.md#black-and-white)) and judge the converted base before adding contrast.\n2. The mix is the main control: name the colour families of the subject and its surroundings on the colour version, then move each family's `GrayMixer*` key to separate them (darken green and yellow to sink foliage behind a pale animal, darken blue for a deeper sky, lighten orange for skin or fur). Move in steps of about 15–25. Strong opposite moves on neighbouring hues tear smooth gradients and raise noise: check skies in a detail crop.\n3. Then tone: contrast, curve, local light; grain only as a chosen quality.\n4. Fringes: Defringe does not act here ([tools](references/tools.md#write-develop-settings)); a colour fringe becomes a light or dark edge whose brightness depends on the mix.\n\n## Make a preset from a tuned look\n\nTune the look on the representative through Lightroom's render, then save only the look's components: curves, HSL and colour relationships, Color Grading, an intentionally chosen profile, grain or vignette when they are part of the style. Usually leave out Exposure, white balance, crop and transform, lens corrections, masks and healing, sharpening and Denoise: they depend on the scene, camera, geometry and output.\n\n- `create_develop_preset` saves the chosen settings as a versioned plug-in checkpoint (hidden from Lightroom's preset panel); give each version a unique name. `compare_develop_presets` diffs it against an approved earlier preset (read one with `get_develop_preset`, by `preset_uuid` when names repeat). `export_develop_preset` writes the file without overwriting.\n- A preset the person can see in the Develop panel is made in Lightroom's own New Preset dialog, ticking only the look's groups; File > Import Develop Profiles and Presets installs exported files.\n- Before calling a preset done: apply it to test copies (`apply_develop_preset`, with `dry_run` first), read back the persisted settings and look at the renders. Report how far you validated it: saved; imported and visible in Lightroom; rendered and judged on named photos; portable within a stated scope (a typical frame, an extreme of the expected light and an expected failure case). A preset that needs major rescue on most targets is badly scoped: divide it by light instead of adding exceptions.\n- A preset with AI masks recomputes them on each target, and a target whose masks fail keeps the preset and is `unknown` ([masks](references/masks.md#ai-masks-judged-on-the-photo)); verify their edges per image.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Julieanne Kost: Color Grading](https://jkost.com/blog/?p=18167); [Max Wendt: introducing Color Grading](https://blog.adobe.com/en/publish/2020/10/20/introducing-color-grading).\n- [Lightroom Queen: what's new in Lightroom Classic 16.0](https://www.lightroomqueen.com/whats-new-in-lightroom-2026-11/): Glow styles and per-mask limits, Projection.\n- [PHLEARN / Aaron Nace: creating presets](https://phlearn.com/tutorial/lightroom-classic-presets/): choose components, leave the hero frame's white balance out.\n- [Ann and Steve Toon: black and white wildlife](https://www.naturettl.com/black-and-white-safari-photography/).\n- [Adobe: apply presets](https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/apply-presets.html).\n\n\n# references/masks.md\n\n# Masks\n\nUse a mask when a global change cannot do the job: a subject that needs light the background must not get, a sky that needs restraint, an eye that needs a touch of clarity. Every mask is checked on the render, at its edges, before you go on.\n\n## Addressing and vocabulary\n\n- Masks and their components are addressed by the ids `list_masks` returns (masks, and per mask its tools with `type`, `subtype`, `inverted`), or by the ids a creating call returns. Edits can regenerate ids: use the latest ones.\n- Sliders use one vocabulary everywhere: the `local_*` SDK names (`local_Exposure` −4..4, `local_Contrast`, `local_Highlights`, `local_Shadows`, `local_Texture`, `local_Clarity`, `local_Dehaze`, `local_Sharpness`, `local_LuminanceNoise`, `local_Defringe`, `local_colorgrade*`, `local_glow`, …), in the `adjustments` of every creating call and in `set_mask_adjustments`. Each range is in the schema; `describe_develop_keys` with a `mask_id` shows where Lightroom's live range differs.\n- A new geometry mask without `adjustments`, and every new People or Landscape mask, applies no local adjustment until `set_mask_adjustments`, though on a RAW its creation can trigger a re-render ([below](references/masks.md#mask-writes-re-render-a-raw)).\n- Background components appear as `aiSelection` / `object` in `list_masks`; that is expected.\n\n## Values: read and written in the saved settings\n\n`get_mask`, `set_mask_adjustments`, `set_mask_curves` and every action of `edit_mask_point_colors` work on the photo's saved settings by mask id, without opening Develop or selecting the mask.\n\n- A write changes only the named sliders, channels or swatches and is read back exactly; a changed value adds one history step, and a request with nothing to change can write nothing. If Lightroom changed anything else or stored another value, the photo is put back and the call fails naming what Lightroom stored.\n- The reply gives stored values: sliders to six decimals, colour-grade sliders as whole numbers (a hue of 212.4 is stored as 212), Point Color to six decimals.\n- **A value write turns the mask on**, as Lightroom's own sliders do: a mask `list_masks` shows as `hidden` is shown again, with a warning. Check before writing to a mask the person switched off.\n- Mask curves take at most 16 points; a straight `[0,0,255,255]` is stored as no curve, and a written channel replaces that channel's extended (HDR) curve.\n- Point Color: `add` needs `SrcHue`, `SrcSat`, `SrcLum`; like Lightroom's own add it stores the source rounded to two decimals and gives the other fields Lightroom's defaults. A mask holds at most eight swatches, with no repeated source colour; `delete` moves later swatches up. Each range's edges must be ordered (LowerNone ≤ LowerFull ≤ UpperFull ≤ UpperNone), because Lightroom drops every swatch of the mask otherwise.\n- Slider changes the person has pending in Develop on that photo are committed first. A read gives the saved state, which can trail a change made by hand a few seconds earlier.\n- `delete_masks` removes masks or top-level components from the saved settings in one history step; `delete_empty_masks` removes masks with no tools over many photos, and keeps every mask that has a component, adjusted or not.\n\nThese value edits construct nothing, so they need no certification.\n\n## Constructing writes need admission\n\nWrites that construct mask data in Lightroom's undocumented format are guarded: `create_gradient_mask`, `update_gradient_mask`, `create_brush_mask`, `add_brush_strokes`, `create_range_mask`, `create_mask`, `create_mask_batch`, `combine_mask`, and a full `copy_develop_settings` that carries masks.\n\n- They run on the throwaway catalog of the project's live test kit. On any other catalog they need a certification run on that machine that passed for the running Lightroom version and build and the photo's process version.\n- `get_mask_write_admission` (with `photo_id`) reports admitted or not and why, without writing.\n- A refusal names the reason; after a Lightroom update the guarded writes refuse until certification is rerun. Report that to the person instead of working around it.\n- They check what they construct against the certified format, leave every other mask as Lightroom stored it, read the whole mask list back and roll back on a mismatch.\n- `list_masks` and `invert_mask` still act in Develop on the active photo. They wait up to 5 s until Develop lists as many masks as the saved settings hold, and fail with nothing changed while it lists fewer: the masks are not loaded yet, or an alert such as \"No sky detected\" is open from the person's own work. Click OK in Lightroom if an alert is open, then call again. An empty `list_masks` means the photo holds no masks.\n\n## Positions\n\nGradient, brush and range positions are normalised 0..1 in the frame of the rendered preview, as `get_photo_preview` shows it: after crop, straighten and displayed orientation, origin top-left, y down. Read them straight off a preview; the tools convert to the frame Lightroom stores and answer in the preview frame. Gradient widths and heights are fractions of the preview's width and height, brush radius a fraction of its longer edge (default 0.14; dabs are spaced at most half a radius apart).\n\nLightroom stores masks before the crop, so a mask stays on the same part of the photo when the crop changes; re-read a mask's geometry after a crop change before reshaping it. With Upright, a manual Transform or manual distortion active, a position can land up to about 0.05 of the frame away near the edges (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0); the write answers with a warning: check the render and move the geometry by the offset you see.\n\nRange masks: luminance and depth bands at least 0.01 wide (depth needs a photo with a depth map); a colour range takes exactly one sample (a second one crashed Lightroom), given as Lightroom's own per-photo l, a, b rankings, not sRGB: read them with `list_masks` from a colour range made in Lightroom, or copy one with `copy_sample_from`.\n\n## AI masks judged on the photo\n\nFour calls write AI masks: `create_mask` (Subject, Sky, Background, People parts, Landscape categories), `create_mask_batch` (one Subject, Sky or Background mask on up to 200 photos), `combine_mask` (add, subtract or intersect a Subject, Sky or Background component) and a full copy with AI masks. Each writes the new mask into the saved settings, lets Lightroom compute it with one Update AI Settings, and judges its own masks on the photo:\n\n- computed: `done`;\n- Lightroom reports finding nothing, or could not compute: the new masks are removed, the photo's own masks are restored exactly, and the call fails naming the failed components; no alert needs dismissing;\n- if that same compute also refreshed other settings (stale Lens Blur depth, Enhance filters, Remove areas, an adaptive profile), those are not rolled back: the call is `unknown` and names them, and the masks are as before;\n- anything on the photo still computing 15 s later: `unknown`, and nothing more may be written to that photo until a read shows the compute finished;\n- a preset is the exception: `apply_develop_preset` keeps the preset and answers `unknown` (check the masks in Develop before writing to the photo), and an apply that stops ends the batch with the rest in `remaining`;\n- existing AI masks are never written, but the compute covers the whole photo: a warning names an existing mask whose result changed or was already failed. Render it before going on.\n\n**Done is not found.** Lightroom does not always report an empty result (a Sky on a frame without sky computed a mask, observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0), and a computed mask can miss what matters. Verify every AI mask by giving it an obvious adjustment and comparing previews, or by its first real adjustment. An AI mask was in the preview about a second after the call (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0); if a mask shows no effect, fetch the preview again before concluding.\n\n`combine_mask` worked in all three forms on a dense subject with Exposure −1 (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0): add darkened the subject like Subject alone, subtract left the subject and darkened the rest, intersect darkened only the overlap. Intersect is stored as a subtracted, inverted component. People parts: each part becomes its own mask, and there is no single-person choice (Lightroom masks the first person).\n\n`invert_mask` `in_place` flips one component; `copy` makes Lightroom's inverted duplicate and writes every value of the source onto it. Use the returned `mask_id`.\n\n## Mask writes re-render a RAW\n\nOn a RAW, the first gradient or brush mask, even one without adjustments, can make Lightroom re-render with a fine texture-level difference, and so can a parametric mask write on a photo that already holds an AI mask. The difference touched a fifth to a third of the pixels by more than 0.01 of full scale, with a mean near zero and no region (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0, not on non-RAW files). After a mask change, wait for a fresh stable preview and compare regional tone as well as detail; do not assume that every unexpected change has this cause. Judge sharpening and noise reduction on the final state.\n\n## Check every mask on the render\n\nLook at the edges the mask had to find: hair, whiskers, feathers, fur against foliage, glass, thin branches against sky, the gap between a limb and the body. A successful selection or a mask overlay does not prove the final edge. Look for halos, a cut-out look, colour contamination and a background mask that treats very different areas the same. For a batch, inspect representatives plus every failed or exceptional target; do not substitute reading slider values for looking.\n\n\n# references/portrait-product.md\n\n# Portrait, product and food\n\nApply the [foundations](references/foundations.md), then choose how truthful the picture must be and which materials matter.\n\n## People\n\nFirst choose what the picture is about: contact, character, a relationship, or the person in a place. Compare expressions, gesture, head angle, catchlights and distracting overlaps before pixel-level work. Closed eyes, movement and shallow depth of field can be intentional; reject them only when they defeat this picture's purpose.\n\nSkin lives mostly in the orange HSL family and the red curve, so a look that pushes red midtones also reaches skin. Balance skin against its surroundings and the actual light; a consistently orange face is no evidence of correct white balance. Mixed light can keep its character when the main face convinces. A People mask (`create_mask` with `type: \"people\"` and `parts` such as `face_skin`, `iris_and_pupil`, `hair`, `teeth`) can isolate parts, but it always takes the first person and its edge must survive hair, brows, nostrils, teeth, glasses and clothing. Protect identity and expression: detailed eye, skin or body retouching needs an actual request, not an automatic beauty standard.\n\nTexture and Clarity treat skin differently from eyes and hair. Reduce distracting texture with restraint, keeping pores and transitions; plastic skin is not an improvement. Brightened eyes and teeth must stay believable in the scene's light. A tight portrait strengthens expression at the cost of place: compare both.\n\n## Product and food\n\nEstablish the task: catalogue accuracy, editorial atmosphere or personal interpretation. For accuracy protect packaging and brand colours, material identity, readable labels and shape. Accurate colour needs a reference in the shot (a grey card or colour target) and ideally a custom camera profile; without one, colour accuracy is judgment, not measurement. A mood grade that changes the product's colour has a real cost even when it looks good.\n\nInspect the whole object and its contour: handle, base, rim, contact shadow, reflections. A specular highlight can describe gloss or metal and need not hold texture; decide whether it is useful before reducing every highlight. Do not neutralise deliberate warm food light into grey or erase the grounding shadow so the object floats. Check white surfaces for green or magenta contamination and saturated food for false hues or clipped channels. Separate object and background adjustments when their needs differ; Texture can clarify stone, ceramic and food but coarsen sauces, skin or smooth packaging. Changing product geometry or material is a separate creative decision. Small text, glass edges, thin stems and reflective boundaries need a detail crop.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Karl Taylor: Clinique post-production](https://visualeducation.com/class/clinique-shoot-post-production-2-cleaning-bottles-creating-reflections/): material reads through reflected light and transitions (Photoshop retouching, not a Lightroom route).\n- [PHLEARN: skin tones and colour grading](https://phlearn.com/tutorial/skin-tones-color-grading-photoshop-lightroom/): correction before grading, per-image exposure and white balance when synchronising.\n- [Mareike Keicher: colour harmonies](https://www.captureone.com/blog/applying-color-harmonies-in-capture-one): colours that cannot change.\n\nResearch a specialist when a request goes beyond these principles: beauty retouching, commercial colour tolerances, jewellery or glass, food styling, a named artist's method.\n\n\n# references/publishing.md\n\n# Publishing to a destination\n\nFrom \"prepare these for Instagram\" to verified files. Preparing ends in exported files in a folder the person uploads from; posting is the person's own act. Do not change the masters' edits, ratings, flags or collections unless asked.\n\n## Delivery facts\n\nExamples; add the destination the person names, and check its current limits, since platforms change their formats (first-party pages, checked November 2026).\n\n| Destination | Ratios (width:height) | Delivery size |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Instagram feed | app 1.91:1 to 3:4; publishing API 1.91:1 to 4:5; other ratios are cropped | 1080 px wide (the API accepts up to 1440 and converts to sRGB) |\n| Pinterest | 2:3 recommended; taller pins may be cut off in feeds | 1000×1500 for 2:3, 1000 px wide otherwise |\n\nUpload routes may resize or recompress the image: suggest posting one file privately first and checking the downloaded result.\n\n## 1. Read the final crop\n\nRead each photo's crop with `get_crop`: rectangle, angle and the cropped frame's `size` in pixels. Its ratio r = width ÷ height of that size. Do not compute size or ratio from the raw crop keys: with a straighten angle or a vertical frame they mislead.\n\n## 2. Choose the format\n\nA format fits when a box of its ratio inside the final crop (full height for a narrower format, full width for a wider one) contains the whole subject (with its reflection when that is part of the picture), cuts no second subject, and leaves room on the side the subject faces or moves. Plan the box on a preview of the final edit. For a destination that accepts several ratios, choose the accepted ratio that best preserves the composition; a taller format takes more of a feed's screen, and a common series ratio only when the person wants one. When nothing fits, say which subjects each format would cut instead of forcing one.\n\n## 3. Make a variant only when the format needs a new crop\n\n1. Make a virtual copy of the master named for the format (`create_virtual_copies` with `copy_name`, such as `IG 4x5`); it inherits the edit, crop and masks.\n2. Inside the final crop (the default): on the copy, `set_crop` with `relative_to: \"crop\"`, the format as `aspect` and the planned box's `center` and `scale`, placed on the ordinary preview.\n3. Outside the final crop only when the format needs room the crop lacks and that area holds nothing the person cropped out on purpose, by the re-crop route in [tools](references/tools.md#crop).\n4. Check a fresh preview of the copy: the ratio, the whole subject and its room, nothing the final crop had removed on purpose, and mask effects still on the right areas (Lightroom stores masks before the crop, so they stay on the same part of the photo). Otherwise restore the kept crop on the copy and report the format as not made.\n5. Add the copy to a collection when the person keeps variants that way; `add_to_collection` reports `already_in_collection` for photos already there.\n\n## 4. Gates before export\n\n- The crop is at least the delivery size in both dimensions, without enlargement (the `size` from `get_crop` or `set_crop`).\n- The technical gate holds at that destination's delivery size; a tighter variant enlarges the subject on screen, so check its critical detail again ([cull](references/cull.md#the-procedure)).\n\n## 5. Export and verify\n\nCall `export_photos` with the master's or the copy's id, `format: \"jpeg\"`, an explicit `quality` (90–95 for web), the delivery `width`/`height` as maxima, `on_existing: \"skip\"` and a new destination folder. Without an export preset the plug-in exports sRGB and never enlarges, and it sets no output sharpening: state sharpening as unknown unless a preset sets it. Export a few photos per call, one call at a time; after an error inspect the folder before retrying.\n\nCheck every written file: one per photo, JPEG, the expected pixel size and an embedded sRGB profile. Report per photo the format chosen and, for a new crop, the copy id and what was given up; frames that do not suit the destination and why; the folder and file sizes; and what was not verified (full-resolution mask edges, output sharpening, how the platform renders the upload).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Instagram Help: photo sizes](https://help.instagram.com/1631821640426723); [Instagram Content Publishing API](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/instagram-platform/instagram-graph-api/reference/ig-user/media).\n- [Pinterest product specs](https://help.pinterest.com/en/business/article/pinterest-product-specs).\n\n\n# references/tools.md\n\n# Tools: mechanics that decide whether a call does what you meant\n\nRead the tool's own description before a call you have not made in this session; it states arguments, rules and outcomes exactly. This file adds how the tools fit together and what has been observed.\n\n## Connection, toolsets and read-only mode\n\n- The server talks to a plug-in inside a running Lightroom Classic. On a connection error read its text: check that Lightroom runs with the plug-in enabled, that ports match and the token is readable. Several clients normally share one bridge; an owner that stops answering needs diagnosis. Tell the person instead of retrying in a loop.\n- Toolsets: core is always on; `develop`, `masks` or `all` add the rest. They are fixed at server start, so a changed setting needs a restarted server and does not add tools to a running session.\n- Read-only mode hides and refuses every write, including exports, `export_photo_metadata`, `export_develop_preset` and `get_photo_detail`. Reads that use Develop (`list_masks`, `describe_develop_keys`, `get_lens_blur`, `list_spots`) can still switch the module, photo or selected mask briefly, then restore the view; they change no photo data.\n- `undo_last_step` undoes the last shared history step, which may be the person's or another plug-in's, and Lightroom can merge recent plug-in writes into one step. Use a snapshot or a recorded value to go back instead.\n\n## Identify photos\n\n- Ids are catalog-local strings in every reply; an input takes the id (number or digit string) or an absolute file path. Prefer ids: a path names the master, never a virtual copy.\n- A virtual copy keeps its master's filename. `get_photo_metadata` reports `is_virtual_copy` and `virtual_copy` (`master_photo_id`, `copy_name`, or a master's `copy_photo_ids`), and `stack` (`position`, `is_collapsed`, `top_photo_id`, `member_photo_ids`).\n- `search_photos` does not see the hidden members of a collapsed stack; its warning names the stack tops on the page. Read a top's `member_photo_ids` to reach them.\n- Follow pagination (`limit`, `offset`, `has_more`) until the set is complete.\n\n## Outcomes, ordering and time\n\nThe outcome table is in [SKILL.md](SKILL.md#read-every-replys-outcome). In addition:\n\n- `failed` covers every problem found before anything was written: a broken argument rule (each description lists its \"Rules:\", answered as \"Invalid arguments for …\"), an unknown id, a value out of range, a rule between develop keys. Fix the argument; nothing needs repair.\n- `unknown` names what was written (\"written before this stopped: …\"). In a batch each photo is classed by what was written for it. A tool that wrote in Develop and then put things back (an inverted mask copy, a spot update, Denoise) still answers `unknown`: read the photo before retrying.\n- Develop calls queue in arrival order across every client; a call whose turn does not come within its budget fails without effect. A call that timed out keeps the turn until Lightroom finishes it or 60 s pass; after that it can overlap the next call and stays `unknown`. Send one call, wait for its reply.\n- A catalog write waits up to 10 s while Lightroom or another plug-in writes; a busy catalog fails a call that had written nothing.\n- Batch tools stop starting photos before their time budget ends and list the rest in `remaining`; each description states its budget. Resend exactly `remaining`; never restart the whole list.\n- Tools that open a photo in Develop restore the person's module, view source and selection afterwards. Reads that open Develop also commit a slider change the person is making at that moment, so do not run them while the person is editing that photo.\n\n## Read the state\n\n- `get_develop_settings` is the one develop read, by the same SDK names `set_develop_settings` writes; `keys` is required for more than one photo, and `absent` lists keys a photo does not carry. A `ToneCurvePV2012*` channel reads the curve Lightroom renders (its extended HDR curve when there is one); name `ExtendedToneCurvePV2012*` to read that field as stored.\n- `describe_develop_keys` lists each key's kind, the range the tools enforce and Lightroom's live range for this photo, marked `differs` where they disagree; local slider ranges need a `mask_id`. Observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0 (on a RAW): the three `Parametric*Split` keys live at 10..70, 20..80 and 30..90 while the published range is 0..100. Keep values inside the live range; a value Lightroom stores differently comes back in `adjusted`.\n- `get_crop` reads the crop as `set_crop` states it: the rectangle in the photo as displayed before any crop, the straighten angle and the cropped frame's size in pixels. It opens no Develop.\n- `summarize_develop_settings` gives medians and ranges by camera and ISO for rated photos, useful to learn a person's habitual base before proposing values.\n\n## Write develop settings\n\n- `set_develop_settings` refuses a value outside its range before writing; Lightroom would otherwise clamp it or reset the setting to its default.\n- It also refuses combinations that break a rule: crop edges, each Defringe hue pair at least 10 apart, `GlowStyle` only with Glow above 0, `GlowDirection` only with style 3 or 4, `GrayMixer*` only on a black and white photo, point curves of 2 to 16 points starting at input 0, ending at 255 with rising inputs.\n- A number finer than Lightroom keeps is rounded, with a warning.\n- About a second after the write the tool reads the keys back. A value stored differently is listed in `adjusted` and the outcome is `partial`; a key the photo held and lost makes it `unknown`. A key the photo cannot store at all, such as a RAW's Temperature and Tint on a JPEG, is listed in `not_stored` with a warning.\n- Never write the raw `Crop*` keys: they are in the frame Lightroom stores, not as the photo is displayed. Use `set_crop`. Never invent key names: the schema lists every key the tool accepts.\n- Colour Grading's shadow and highlight wheels are stored under the `SplitToning*` names (`SplitToningShadowHue`, `SplitToningHighlightSaturation`, `SplitToningBalance`); midtones, global and blending under `ColorGrade*`.\n- Keys that need their own tool: `ConvertToGrayscale` (`set_treatment`), Denoise (`apply_denoise`), Lens Blur (`set_lens_blur`), process version (`set_process_version`), crop (`set_crop`). Profile selection has no tool: use a preset that carries the profile, or Lightroom's UI.\n\nGroups observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0 (range, read-back and a render that changed as expected, on test images):\n\n| Group | Keys |\n| --- | --- |\n| Chromatic aberration | `AutoLateralCA` 0/1 (Remove Chromatic Aberration); `LensProfileEnable` 0/1 |\n| Defringe | `DefringePurpleAmount`, `DefringeGreenAmount` 0..20; hue ranges `DefringePurpleHueLo/Hi`, `DefringeGreenHueLo/Hi`. Renders nothing on a black and white photo |\n| Color Grading | as above; hues 0..360 (about 45 amber, 120 green, 220 blue, 300 magenta), saturations 0..100, luminances and balance −100..100, `ColorGradeBlending` 0..100 (default 50) |\n| Black and white mix | `GrayMixerRed`, `GrayMixerOrange`, `GrayMixerYellow`, `GrayMixerGreen`, `GrayMixerAqua`, `GrayMixerBlue`, `GrayMixerPurple`, `GrayMixerMagenta` −100..100 |\n| Upright and transform | `PerspectiveUpright` 0–4 ([modes](references/architecture-landscape.md#upright-and-projection)); `PerspectiveVertical`, `PerspectiveHorizontal`, `PerspectiveAspect`, `LensManualDistortionAmount` −100..100; `PerspectiveRotate` −10..10; `PerspectiveScale` 50..150 |\n| Glow | `Glow`, `GlowRange`, `GlowSpread` 0..100, `GlowWarmth` −100..100, `GlowStyle` 0 diffusion, 1 bloom, 2 halation, 3 motion, 4 streak, `GlowDirection` −180..180 |\n| Projection | `ReshapeAmount` 0..100 |\n\nNot settable and not to be invented: legacy `ChromaticAberrationR/B` and `Defringe` (they read back and render nothing), profile, Upright Guided.\n\n## Crop\n\n- `set_crop` takes either a `rectangle` (left, top, right, bottom, 0..1) or an `aspect` ({width, height}, such as 4:5 or 1.91:1) with the largest frame that fits around a `center`, optionally reduced by `scale`, each with an optional straighten `angle` (−45..45; positive turns the frame clockwise, so the picture turns counter-clockwise).\n- `relative_to: \"photo\"` (default) means the photo as displayed before any crop; `relative_to: \"crop\"` means the current crop as a preview shows it, and the angle adds to the current one.\n- Before writing, the tool refuses a frame that would leave the photo (naming the largest that fits), a tall or wide frame whose turned corners would cross, and a total angle outside ±45. With Upright, Transform or manual distortion active the frame can sit up to about 0.05 of the photo away near the edges, with a warning.\n- To re-crop: read and keep `get_crop`. A tighter frame inside the current crop is one `set_crop` with `relative_to: \"crop\"`, placed on an ordinary preview. A frame that needs area outside the crop needs the whole photo first, because previews show only the current crop. Set `set_crop` with rectangle 0, 0, 1, 1 and angle 0 (a full rectangle at any other angle leaves the photo and is refused) and look at a fresh preview. Then write the new frame with `relative_to: \"photo\"` and the intended angle; the tool checks that it fits. That preview is unrotated, so place the new frame's centre and size on it and let the angle turn the frame around that centre. To undo, write the kept rectangle and angle back.\n\n## Black and white\n\n`set_treatment` switches between `color` and `black_and_white` through Lightroom's own conversion. On a RAW it swaps the profile (Adobe Color to Adobe Monochrome) and removes or restores the HSL keys (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0: also Saturation and Vibrance, and Monochrome brings its own tone curve and a little Clarity); the reply reports `convert_to_grayscale` and `profile {before, after}`. No tool writes a profile back, so a profile the conversion lost cannot be restored through the tools: convert a virtual copy rather than a master whose profile matters. `match_photo_style` fails a target whose treatment differs from the reference; convert it first.\n\n## Snapshots, virtual copies, history\n\n- Snapshots are stored per master: a virtual copy's snapshots are listed on its master and every copy, and deleting one through a copy deletes it for all. `apply_develop_snapshot` changes only the photo named. Name a snapshot after the photo and the moment (filename plus \"before grading\") and check the name before applying or deleting.\n- `create_virtual_copies` names each copy `copy_name`, returns `copies` (`master_photo_id`, `copy_photo_id`) and keeps copies made before a failure in its data. A new copy joins its master's stack (observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0). Edit through `copy_photo_id`. Also observed on Lightroom Classic 16.0: the call occasionally fails inside Lightroom without creating anything; read the master's `copy_photo_ids` before one retry.\n- `match_photo_style` makes a recovery snapshot per target before writing; `apply_develop_snapshot` restores it.\n\n## Presets, copying and automatic adjustments\n\n- `copy_develop_settings` with a `settings` list copies only those keys and leaves the target's masks and process version alone (`ProcessVersion` cannot be named); without one it copies everything including masks, which then replace the target's masks through the guarded mask write (and a source without masks removes the target's masks). A full copy leaves out Enhance results (Denoise, Raw Details, Super Resolution); a RAW's white balance does not reach a non-RAW target (`not_stored`); a target on another process version first gets the source's, as its own history step. Use `dry_run` first; it lists what would change. For a set, always name the keys.\n- `apply_develop_preset` and `copy_develop_settings` compute AI masks on each target ([masks](references/masks.md#ai-masks-judged-on-the-photo)).\n- `match_photo_style` transfers the global look (tone, colour, curves, detail, noise, grain, vignette) and leaves out what describes one photo: crop, perspective and Upright, white balance, lens corrections, local adjustments.\n- Saving, comparing and exporting presets: [looks](references/looks.md#make-a-preset-from-a-tuned-look).\n- `apply_auto_adjustments` gives no completion signal: a photo where no change is seen within 8 s is `unknown`; read its settings before writing to it.\n- `apply_denoise` computes in the background; \"applied\" does not mean every preview has caught up. An unsaved change answers `unknown`: read the Denoise state before retrying.\n- `set_process_version` can change the look and refuses downgrades of photos with masks; `reset_develop_adjustments` removes every edit, crop and masks included. Both need the person's word.\n\n## Previews and detail crops\n\n- `get_photo_preview` returns one render, whole or a `region` of the rendered frame (after crop, straighten and orientation). Named sizes: `cull` (864 px, the default) and `review` (1728 px); a number 64–2048 asks for exactly that edge. `get_photo_previews` returns up to 20 photos (default edge 256).\n- `width`/`height` are the pixels sent; the server never enlarges, and `scaled_by_server` only means Lightroom answered larger and the server reduced it. Lightroom's preview cache can still hand over an enlargement of a smaller preview without saying so (seen as softness at both named sizes on a cropped RAW); what that means for judging is in [SKILL.md](SKILL.md#judge-pictures-on-the-right-evidence). Right after a change Lightroom sometimes has no preview pixels; the call asks again for about 20 s and then fails: ask again later, and check whether the photo is the one active in Develop.\n- `get_photo_detail` cuts one to four regions (normalised in the displayed, cropped frame) from a full-resolution render with the current settings, one rendered pixel per returned pixel. `max_edge` defaults to 1024 (at most 2048); keep it within what your model sees without downscaling. A region larger than `max_edge` is refused before rendering with the largest normalised size this photo allows: shrink to it. Each call adds one Export history step and the exported mark (so `summarize_develop_settings`' `exported_only` counts it); settings and pixels stay unchanged ([SKILL.md](SKILL.md#judge-pictures-on-the-right-evidence) for one call per photo). It shares a lock with `export_photos`: while one runs, the other is refused, not queued.\n- `get_photo_stats` and `find_duplicate_groups` measure previews: useful to flag frames, never to rank sharpness or prove clipping. Duplicate groups are not reject lists, and similarity says nothing about which frame is better.\n\n## Exports\n\n- `export_photos` writes files: give a deliberate, new `destination` folder or a usable export preset (`list_export_presets`), `format`, an explicit `quality` and `on_existing` (`skip` or `rename`; `overwrite` is refused into catalog folders). `width`/`height` are maxima; Lightroom does not enlarge.\n- The reply lists the written `files`; inspect those files, not the folder listing. A photo whose export session failed after it started is `unknown` and may carry an Export history step. Export in small batches, one call at a time, and inspect the folder before any retry.\n- Keep exports to what the task needs and say where they are; never bulk-export a catalog to look at it.\n\n## Annotations and metadata\n\n- `set_rating_and_flag` (rating 0–5, flag `pick`, `reject` or `none`), `set_color_label` (`red`, `yellow`, `green`, `blue`, `purple`, `none`). Reads report the same words.\n- `set_ai_metadata` stores a private score and reason per photo in the catalog, invisible to IPTC, keywords and exports: the place for your reasons. Read `get_ai_metadata` before replacing earlier notes.\n- `set_keywords` with a dedicated parent (such as `AI Keywords`) keeps a tagging batch removable with `remove_keyword_subtree`.\n\n\n# references/wildlife.md\n\n# Birds and animals\n\nCombine the foundations with the animal's behaviour, the recorded light and the detail that matters; do not turn every animal into the same isolated portrait.\n\n## Cull the moment\n\nCompare same-animal sequences, not detector pairs or single thumbnails. Run the technical gate on every frame of a sequence first, with eye crops at one magnification ([cull](references/cull.md#the-procedure)), then apply the weak-sequence rule. Weigh differences in gaze, behaviour, wing and body shape, interaction and habitat. Keep four judgments apart: a technical failure that defeats the subject at the intended size; a weaker usable alternative; uncertain; a candidate with a clear development opportunity.\n\nFor action, check the eye first, then behaviour, then wing, leg and prey shape and overlaps, then background and crop. A sharp head with softer moving wings can succeed. Closed eyes can express rest; noise and shallow depth of field are not automatic failures. Image stabilisation addresses camera movement, not the animal's.\n\n## Compose subject, habitat and water\n\nChoose portrait, action, interaction or animal-in-environment. Keep the space the gaze, movement, wing, tail and reflection need rather than a fixed eye position. A tight crop costs context and magnifies noise and softness; keep the crop at or above the delivery size and judge a frame of marginal sharpness at delivery scale ([cull](references/cull.md#the-procedure)). Quiet a competing background without a cut-out look; soft foreground can add depth when key anatomy still reads.\n\nWater is a material: judge droplets, reflections, ripples and smooth water separately. Strong sharpening makes ripples brittle; strong noise reduction makes them plastic. A clear reflection can form one figure with the animal; a distorted one is no straightening reference. A wake can matter more than space in front of the beak. A single background mask need not get one uniform treatment.\n\n## Develop the recorded light\n\nMatch the treatment to the light that is there. Directions to test, not recipes:\n\n| Recorded situation | Direction to test | Cost to check |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Shaded animal, bright water | Open the subject locally, keep the warm reflected light and pale feathers | Noisy head, background dominance, warm cast on white |\n| Side-lit face or body | Support the bright side, quiet distractions | Opening every shadow flattens the light; deep burning makes a cut-out |\n| Soft light against pale water or sky | Airy tones with dark eye and beak anchors | Lost white detail; excess contrast kills the air |\n| Backlit profile or rim | Graphic silhouette or restrained body detail | A silhouette gives up markings; a bright body weakens the rim |\n| A real patch of forest light | Keep the light niche inside deep surroundings | Empty black habitat, or the mood lost by lifting all shadows |\n| Soft foreground foliage | Keep a gentle depth frame, lower its contrast and colour | Added-looking haze, hidden anatomy |\n\nCheck feather, fur, whisker and limb-gap edges without an overlay. Keep important markings and white or black detail unless losing them is a chosen trade-off. For white plumage lower Highlights and Whites globally and in a subject mask, then check clipping on the render at delivery size. Check bright edges for purple and green fringes in a detail crop. Remove Chromatic Aberration first; use Defringe only on evidence, starting low (about 4 of 20) and raising in small steps while reading the same crop. Check that it does not desaturate genuinely purple, magenta or green plumage, flowers or leaf edges; a negative `local_Defringe` in a subject mask protects them. Global green HSL changes a green frog with its leaves; a background mask or a luminance separation may serve better.\n\n## Detail\n\nInspect the eye or head, fine fur or feather structure and a smooth area before accepting noise reduction ([development](references/development.md#detail-without-damage)). On the eye, enhance a catchlight that exists only modestly; never add one. In monochrome, verify that animal and habitat do not merge in grey.\n\n## Capture lessons\n\nDiagnose the cause of a failure only to improve the next shoot. In low light distinguish motion from stillness: motion may justify a higher ISO for a useful shutter speed; a still animal allows support, a slower shutter and several attempts. Do not underexpose to show a low ISO. Through a fence, a lens close to the mesh, a distant animal and a wide aperture reduce it; Dehaze can reveal residual mesh, and removing bars over an animal reconstructs content rather than recovers it. Never approach or cross barriers for a picture.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [birdphotography.com: sharp images](https://birdphotography.com/articles/achieving-sharp-images-troubleshooting-and-techniques/): eye-first sharpness, shutter-speed priors.\n- [Feathered Photography: catchlights](https://featheredphotography.com/blog/2021/02/19/catch-lights-in-the-eyes-are-they-overrated/).\n- [Brad James: bird light](https://www.naturettl.com/how-to-harness-light-in-bird-photography/); [Kevin Morgans: backlighting](https://www.naturettl.com/backlighting-wildlife-photography/); [Mike Atkinson: birds and water](https://mikeatkinson.net/Tutorial-8-Birds-and-Water.htm); [Will Nicholls: low light](https://www.naturettl.com/how-to-photograph-wildlife-in-low-light/); [Terry Thomas: zoo photography](https://www.nature-track.com/Zoo_Photography.html).\n- [Julieanne Kost: Denoise in Lightroom Classic 12.3](https://jkost.com/blog/2023/04/denoise-masking-and-edit-in-photoshop-updates-in-lightroom-classic-v12-3.html).";
